   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fe15d340-0e03-4a0e-a6a9-ed752c6c8719",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Feature Engineering output Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Outlier Handling (Winsorization)**\n",
    "   - Applied to `RevolvingUtilizationOfUnsecuredLines`, `DebtRatio`, and `MonthlyIncome`.\n",
    "   - Extreme values were clipped at the 1st and 99th percentiles.\n",
    "   - Purpose: reduce the influence of unrealistic or extreme cases (e.g., very high utilization or missing income).\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Derived Ratios**\n",
    "   - **`Income_per_Dependent`** = `MonthlyIncome` ÷ `NumberOfDependents`  \n",
    "     → Captures financial strain relative to household size. Lower values imply higher vulnerability.\n",
    "   - **`MonthlyDebtProxy`** = `DebtRatio × MonthlyIncome`  \n",
    "     → Converts DebtRatio into an approximate dollar value of monthly debt burden, making it more interpretable.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Delinquency Aggregation**\n",
    "   - **`Total_Delinquencies`** = Sum of all delinquency counts (`30–59`, `60–89`, `90+ days late`).\n",
    "   - Provides a single, comprehensive measure of past credit misbehavior.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **Resulting Dataset**\n",
    "   - New features added: `Income_per_Dependent`, `MonthlyDebtProxy`, `Total_Delinquencies`.\n",
    "   - Some missing values still present (from income/dependents) to be imputed later.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "**Takeaway:**  \n",
    "The dataset now reflects **financial stress** and **credit history** more clearly. These engineered features align with real-world credit underwriting logic, improving both **predictive performance** and **explainability**.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
