   "source": [
    "### Confusion Matrices & Classification Metrics output Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "We compare model predictions at the chosen thresholds (F1-optimal):\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 1. Key Observations\n",
    "- **Random Forest catches more true defaults** (900 vs 776) with fewer missed defaulters (FN = 1105 vs 1229).  \n",
    "- Both models make ~1,100–1,200 false positive errors (approving a risky borrower).  \n",
    "- Precision and Recall both improve slightly with Random Forest:\n",
    "  - **Precision (~42%)** → about 4 in 10 flagged borrowers are truly risky.  \n",
    "  - **Recall (~45%)** → the model identifies nearly half of all actual defaulters.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 2. Business Meaning\n",
    "- **True Positives (TP):** borrowers correctly flagged as risky → would be rejected or priced higher.  \n",
    "- **False Negatives (FN):** risky borrowers mistakenly approved → credit losses.  \n",
    "- **False Positives (FP):** safe borrowers wrongly rejected → opportunity cost (lost revenue + customer dissatisfaction).  \n",
    "- **True Negatives (TN):** safe borrowers correctly approved → ideal outcome.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 3. Trade-offs\n",
    "- Random Forest sacrifices a few more safe borrowers (slightly higher FP) but **catches significantly more risky ones**.  \n",
    "- This is often preferable in credit risk where **avoiding defaults (FN)** matters more than maximizing approvals.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "**Takeaway:**  \n",
    "- **Random Forest > Logistic Regression** for both Recall and overall risk detection.  \n",
    "- In practical terms, Random Forest provides a safer lending policy by catching more defaulters, even at the cost of slightly higher false rejections.\n"
   ]
  },
