    "### Calibration of Predicted Probabilities output Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "Calibration plots compare **predicted default probability** (x-axis) against the **observed default rate** (y-axis).  \n",
    "The orange diagonal line represents *perfect calibration* (predictions match reality).\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 1. Logistic Regression\n",
    "- Predictions are **poorly calibrated**.  \n",
    "- Model tends to **overpredict risk** at higher probability bins (e.g., borrowers with predicted PD of 0.6–0.8 have actual observed default rates closer to 0.2–0.3).  \n",
    "- This gap indicates probabilities cannot be trusted as true likelihoods without post-calibration (e.g., Platt scaling or isotonic regression).\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 2. Random Forest\n",
    "- Much better calibration: predicted probabilities track closer to observed default rates.  \n",
    "- The curve follows the diagonal more closely, especially for low-to-mid risk segments.  \n",
    "- Still slightly underpredicts high-risk bins, but overall more reliable than Logistic Regression.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "**Takeaway:**  \n",
    "- **Random Forest not only discriminates better (AUC/PR) but also produces more realistic probability estimates.**  \n",
    "- Logistic Regression requires calibration to be used for probability-based decisioning.  \n",
    "- In a credit risk setting, Random Forest probabilities could be directly used for risk-based pricing or capital estimation, while Logistic Regression would need post-processing.\n"
