    "\n",
    "### 2. Confusion Matrix Insights\n",
    "- At F1-optimal thresholds:  \n",
    "  - **Random Forest** correctly identified more defaulters (TP = 900 vs 776).  \n",
    "  - It also reduced missed defaults (FN = 1105 vs 1229).  \n",
    "  - Precision ~42% and Recall ~45% show a reasonable balance in an imbalanced dataset.  \n",
    "- For lenders, Random Forest means **fewer bad loans slipping through** at the cost of slightly more false rejections.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. Explainability Findings\n",
    "- **Global Feature Importance:**  \n",
    "  - Key drivers = **credit utilization, delinquency history, age**.  \n",
    "  - Random Forest captures richer **interactions** (e.g., age × delinquency) vs Logistic Regression.  \n",
    "- **PDP/ICE Plots:**  \n",
    "  - Default risk rises sharply with **high utilization** and **any history of delinquency**.  \n",
    "  - **Younger borrowers** face higher risk, stabilizing with age.  \n",
    "- **Logistic Coefficients:**  \n",
    "  - Odds ratios confirm:  \n",
    "    - 3.7× higher risk per SD increase in short-term delinquencies.  \n",
    "    - 2.7× higher risk per SD increase in 90-day delinquencies.  \n",
    "    - ~23% lower risk per SD increase in age.  \n",
    "- Both models align with **credit intuition**: heavy credit usage, past delinquency, and youth drive default risk.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4. Calibration\n",
    "- **Random Forest** → well calibrated: probabilities reflect true default rates.  \n",
    "- **Logistic Regression** → overpredicts at high probabilities, requiring post-calibration.  \n",
    "- For risk-based pricing or regulatory capital estimation, Random Forest is the safer choice.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5. Business Implications\n",
    "- **Operational Use:** Random Forest is a better candidate for deployment, balancing predictive power and reliable probabilities.  \n",
    "- **Policy Design:** Threshold tuning can align with risk appetite — higher threshold (strict approvals) vs lower threshold (higher growth, higher risk).  \n",
    "- **Governance:** Logistic Regression remains valuable as a transparent benchmark with interpretable odds ratios.  \n",
    "- **Explainability:** Feature importance + PDP/ICE plots provide regulators and stakeholders with clear justification for model decisions.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Overall Conclusion\n",
    "- **Random Forest is the superior predictive model** for credit risk in this dataset.  \n",
    "- **Logistic Regression serves as an interpretable baseline** that validates the drivers found by the more powerful Random Forest.  \n",
    "- Together, they demonstrate a balanced approach to **Explainable AI (XAI) in credit risk** — combining predictive accuracy, transparency, and regulator-friendly insights.\n"
   ]
